# Haystack constitution

This is a Unity 6 WebGL game: the player sorts thousands of straws and finds one needle. Full rationale: `Docs/Architecture/ADR-001-haystack-runtime.md`. If a request conflicts with this file, follow this file and say so.

## Hard bans

- Do not give each straw its own GameObject, Transform, MeshRenderer, Rigidbody, Collider, or MonoBehaviour.
- Do not put `Update` / `OnMouse*` / per-straw scripts on hay.
- Do not use `MeshCollider` on a straw. Do not let ECM2 collide with every straw.
- Do not simulate the whole pile with PhysX at runtime.
- Do not use transparent hay materials (overdraw). Prefer opaque or hard alpha clip.
- Do not introduce DOTS/Entities, compute shaders, or `DrawMeshInstancedIndirect` for this system.
- Do not make Jobs/Burst a required path (WebGL is effectively single-threaded).

## Required shape

Hay is data. One `HaystackSystem` (or equivalent manager) owns a `Straw[]` / parallel arrays:

- visual layer — all straws via `Graphics.DrawMeshInstanced` (one low-poly mesh, one instanced material, batches ≤ 1023)
- pick layer — ray vs oriented capsule through a spatial hash; only non-buried surface straws (outer shell of the remaining pile: top and sides, not a top-down height band)
- hot pool — 1–8 real GameObjects for the held straw and recently thrown ones; after settle, write the matrix back and demote

The needle is a flag on one index (`IsNeedle`), not a separate system. Draw it with its own mesh/material from the start (one `DrawMesh`, still not a per-straw GO). Place it near the volume's vertical axis, not on the exposed shell.

Player walks on a separate pile-volume collider that shrinks in height and footprint as hay is removed — never on per-straw colliders.

## When implementing

Read the ADR before adding hay, pickup, pile physics, instancing, or WebGL-related rendering. Change the ADR and this rule together if a decision is overturned; do not silently contradict them in code.
